$ be an eventually increasing sequence of nonnegative integers. Then for each integer $n\\ge 0$ we define the polynomial(1.5)${H}_{n}^{\\mathbf{h}}(z)=\\sum _{j=0}^{n}\\left(\\begin{array}{c}n\\\\ j\\end{array}\\right){z}^{{h}_{j}}.$ For each $m\\ge 1$ we obviously have${H}_{n}^{\\mathbf{h}}(z)={f}_{m,n}(z)\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\text{when}\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}{h}_{j}=\\left(\\begin{array}{c}j\\\\ m\\end{array}\\right).$\n\nIn Section 2 we will derive various basic properties of these polynomials, usually also specialized to the polynomials ${f}_{m,n}(z)$. Section 3 is then devoted to monotonicity properties of the sequence {({H}_{n}^{\\mathbf{h}}(z))}_{n\\ge 0}${({H}_{n}^{\\mathbf{h}}(z))}_{n\\ge 0}$ and to the log-concavity of {({f}_{m,n}(z))}_{n}${({f}_{m,n}(z))}_{n}$. In Section 4 we find some identities connecting the polynomials ${f}_{m,n}(z)$ with their derivatives, and also obtain a partial differential equation satisfied by their generating function. In Section 5 we derive bounds on the moduli of the roots of ${f}_{m,n}(z)$, and actually prove somewhat more general results. We conclude this paper with some remarks and conjectures on the real roots of ${f}_{m,n}(z)$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJ. Math. Anal. Appl. 505 (2022) 125449 (https://ruj.uj.edu.pl/bitstreams/350eeb7c-a395-4b80-a4f7-404281921ee4/download)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; We study an inﬁnite class of sequences of sparse polynomials that have binomial ... After deriving some basic identities, we obtain properties concerning monotonicity ... A sparse polynomial (in one variable) is usually deﬁned to be a polynomial in which the number of nonzero ... Research of the second author supported by a grant of the Polish National Science Centre, no.\nJ. Math. Anal. Appl. 505 (2022) 125449\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect\nJournal of Mathematical Analysis and Applications\nwww.elsevier.com/l